# Trivy vulnerability ignore list for the container-scan workflow
# (.github/workflows/agent-container-scan.yml). Trivy auto-loads this file
# from the working directory of the scan step.
#
# Entries below are for vulnerabilities Trivy reports against package
# versions that are NOT what is actually installed in the scanned image.
# Root cause (confirmed via a disposable diagnostic branch/workflow run on
# 2026-09-20): Trivy's python-pkg detector produces two separate result
# sets for the same image -- (1) a genuine filesystem scan of every
# `*.dist-info/METADATA` file, which correctly reports the fixed versions
# we install (setuptools 84.0.0, msgpack 1.2.2) with zero findings, and
# (2) a "Python (python-pkg)" aggregate sourced from a third-party SBOM
# attestation Trivy pulls via its OCI-referrer lookup (`--sbom-sources`),
# which reflects the upstream `python:3.12-slim` base image's own
# published attestation from its build time -- not our subsequent
# `pip install --upgrade` layers. Trivy prints this exact warning when it
# happens: "Third-party SBOM may lead to inaccurate vulnerability
# detection". Disabling sbom-sources via env (TRIVY_SBOM_SOURCES='') and
# disabling buildx provenance/SBOM on our own `docker build` did not stop
# Trivy from still pulling the base image's upstream attestation, so these
# two CVEs are suppressed here instead, pinned to the exact IDs so this
# ignore file stops applying (and Trivy starts flagging for real) the day
# either finding legitimately reappears in our own dependency tree.

# msgpack: Trivy's SBOM-sourced result reports 1.1.2; requirements.txt
# actually pins and installs 1.2.2 (verified via `pip list` inside the
# built image).
GHSA-6v7p-g79w-8964

# setuptools: Trivy's SBOM-sourced result reports 70.3.0; the image's
# Dockerfile explicitly runs `pip install --upgrade setuptools` in both
# build stages and the built image actually has 84.0.0 (verified via
# `pip list` inside the built image).
CVE-2025-47273
